  "normalizedModelResponse": {
    "episodeSummary": "A 74-year-old female with a history of atrial fibrillation (on sotalol) and hypertension presented with palpitations and syncope. She recently experienced gastroenteritis with vomiting and diarrhea for 3 days and was started on azithromycin and ondansetron 5 days prior. During the episode, she became hypotensive (SBP 68 mmHg) and hypoxic (SpO2 88%). The ECG showed polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (Torsades de pointes) with a rate of 228 bpm, QRS duration of 150 ms, and QT prolongation (QTc 618 ms). Electrolytes revealed hypokalemia (2.9 mmol/L), hypomagnesemia (1.2 mg/dL), and hypocalcemia (7.9 mg/dL).",
    "rhythmInterpretation": "Authoritative upstream diagnosis: Torsades de pointes. Source: SLM_Eval. The rhythm diagnosis was not generated by the SLM.",
    "clinicalContext": "Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (Torsades de pointes) occurred in a patient with underlying structural heart disease (history of atrial fibrillation), recent gastroenteritis with vomiting/diarrhea, and exposure to QT-prolonging medications (sotalol, azithromycin, ondansetron). The event was hemodynamically unstable, associated with hypotension and hypoxia. Pre-event ECG showed marked QT prolongation.",
    "mostLikelyEtiology": "Acquired long QT syndrome secondary to electrolyte abnormalities (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypocalcemia) and QT-prolonging medications (sotalol, azithromycin, ondansetron), likely precipitated by recent gastroenteritis with vomiting and diarrhea leading to electrolyte loss.",
    "contributingFactors": [
      "Hypokalemia (K+ 2.9 mmol/L)",
